<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chamunda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chamundi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chamunda is a fierce form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mother goddess Devi, one of the seven or eight Matrikas. In the Devi Mahatmya she is created from the goddess's anger and slays the demons Chanda and Munda, from whom she takes her name. She is shown as gaunt and terrifying, and is worshipped at many hill shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including Chamundi Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and the hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chamunda.jpg" alt="Chamunda" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Hindu_Goddess_Chamunda_LACMA_M.80.3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ramakrishna Gopal Bhandarkar claims that Chamunda was originally a tribal goddess worshipped by the tribes of the Vindhya mountains in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who offered animal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a> and liquor to goddesses. These methods, he says, were retained in Tantric worship of Chamunda after her assimilation into mainstream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, and the fierce nature of the goddess is due to her association with Rudra, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. Wangu also supports the theory of tribal origins. Chamunda is also counted among the chief Yoginis, the sixty-four or eighty-one Tantric goddesses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the Devi Mahatmya, Chamunda emerged from between the eyebrows of the goddess Kaushiki, who was created from the sheath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, and was charged with killing the demons Chanda and Munda, servants of the demon kings Shumbha and Nishumbha. Another legend has her appear from the frown of Parvati.</p><p>In a later episode of the Devi Mahatmya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> creates the Matrikas, and Kali, who sucks the blood of the demon Raktabija, is given the epithet Chamunda. The Varaha Purana, by contrast, names Chamunda and Kali as separate goddesses, and in the Matsya Purana she and the other Matrikas help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> kill the demon Andhakasura by drinking his blood.</p><p>She is one of the seven Matrikas, fearsome mothers who were emblems of childhood pestilence, fever and disease. She is always shown last in the group, is sometimes called its leader, and is the only Matrika who is a Shakti of the great goddess rather than of a male god and the only one who is worshipped independently.</p><p>In art she is a skeletal old woman with sunken eyes and sagging breasts, black or red, with up to twelve arms holding a drum, trident, sword, snake and skull-bowl, wearing a garland of skulls, and seated on a corpse. This appearance symbolises the inevitability of old age, death and decay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A South Indian inscription describes sacrifices of sheep to Chamunda, and animal sacrifice has become less common with Vaishnavite influences. Chamunda puja is part of the Sandhi Puja, a key ritual of Durga Puja. Temples include the Chamunda Devi Temple near Palampur in Kangra, the Chamundeshwari Temple on Chamundi Hill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, and the shrine on a hilltop at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a>. The Chamunda Mataji temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehrangarh">Mehrangarh Fort</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, was established in 1460 when the ruler Jodha moved the idol from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandore</a>. In Jain legend the monk Ratnaprabhasuri persuaded her to accept vegetarian offerings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osian">Osian</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chamunda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
